  "plot_summary": "A man named El Toro, a Communist-supported candidate, has just been elected president of Santo Rico.&nbsp; Washington summons Giant-Man and the Wasp because they feel the election was won by \"foul means.\"&nbsp; They want the pair to pretend to tour Santo Rico.&nbsp; But El Toro thinks they look suspicious and snags Jan, who has time only to throw a single growth capture Hank's way!\n\nBut being stuck at giant size just makes Hank easier to spot, and the local police chase him.&nbsp; The people are frightened of El Toro, so they keep reporting his whereabouts.&nbsp; Hank hitches a ride on a train to where his bags were sent ahead and at least has the helmet that allows him to communicate with the ants, and they eventually find Jan for him.\n\nHe goes to rescue Jan, gets access again to the pills in her purse, and the pair find the proof in the records of how the election was paid for by the Reds!&nbsp; The citizens are outraged and call him a traitor!&nbsp; The day is saved, and thanks to them, \"Santo Rico is again a democratic nation!\"",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME - write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME - write something",
  "review_text": "Ugh, this book continues to be a major disappointment every time I read it.&nbsp; This was about as lame a plot as there ever was.&nbsp; The story was uninteresting, the \"villain\" a two-dimensional character, and even the action was just going through the motions to fill up pages.&nbsp; Jan kept trying to get Hank to think about going on a vacation, but even that aspect of the story felt crammed in, and in the end, nobody really cared.\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nMy advice is that you skip this one and move on...\n\nNote that the full-page \"Two More Triumphs for Marvel\" page near the back announces [LINK:1964:FF_25|FF #25|2027-05-10] and Daredevil #1, but the text reads \"Dare-Devil\" with the hyphen.&nbsp; It's not stated that they're \"now on sale\" (since I suppose they hadn't yet come out).",
  "side_note": [
    "<b>The Wonderful Wasp Tells a Tale:</b>  \"Conquest\"\n[BR] (by Stan Lee, Larry Lieber, and Sol Brodsky)\n\nJan has to turn down an offer from Hank for a night out because she's promised to watch the son of a sick friend.&nbsp; While she is there, young Tommy asks her to tell him a bedtime story.\n\nThe story involves Shann, the ruler of a \"planet far away.\"&nbsp; The people there are so happy, he fears they will take him for granted and so decides to become \"Shann, the Conqueror\" and orders them to invade a planet on his behalf.\n\nThe invasion goes well but leads to Shann and his people having to support the weaker Andromian people, which causes his own people to resent ever having invaded.&nbsp; Their quality of life has been disrupted because of the extra burden.&nbsp; Shann should have been content to leave things the way they were.\n\n[HANG0] Tommy has only one question for \"Aunt Jan\":\n[BR] \"How come you don't have your <i>wasp's wings</i> when you're normal-sized?\"\n\nJan can only sigh in disbelief...."
